"algs.info.ortegaoll.title" = "About Ortega OLL";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.subtitle" = "Orientation step in the 2x2 Ortega method";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.overview.p1" = "**Ortega OLL** orients the last layer of a 2x2 after the first face is built. It does not require the bottom layer to be fully solved, which is why Ortega stays light and flexible compared with layer-by-layer approaches.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.context.p1" = "Ortega became popular as a practical 2x2 speedsolving method because it asks for only a small algorithm set: build any face, orient the opposite face, then finish with PBL. It is often the first real 2x2 method after beginner solving.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.structure.b1" = "Ortega OLL has **7 cases**";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.structure.b2" = "The goal is only orientation, not full last-layer solving";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.structure.b3" = "Usually followed by **Ortega PBL**";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.structure.b4" = "Low algorithm count makes it a strong first speed method";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.insights.p1" = "The strength of Ortega OLL is that it keeps inspection simple. You only need to plan a face, not a full layer, so many solves become easier to see before the timer starts.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.insights.p2" = "Because the first face pieces may not be permuted, the final solve quality depends heavily on recognizing the PBL that comes next. OLL and PBL should be practiced as one flow rather than two isolated lists.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.insights.p3" = "For many cubers, Ortega OLL is also the bridge into CLL and EG: it teaches the idea of solving orientation first while leaving permutation for a more powerful final step.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on the pattern of oriented stickers on the upper face. Since there are only a few cases, the main skill is choosing the angle that leads naturally into fast PBL recognition.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for anyone moving beyond beginner 2x2. Learn it with PBL, then later decide whether to continue into CLL or EG.";
"algs.info.ortegaoll.quote.p1" = "Ortega OLL is small, but it teaches one of the most important 2x2 habits: build a useful face, then finish from a readable last-layer state.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.title" = "About Ortega PBL";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.subtitle" = "Permute both layers after Ortega orientation";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.overview.p1" = "**Ortega PBL** means **Permutation of Both Layers**. After both faces are oriented, PBL permutes the upper and lower layers at the same time to finish the 2x2.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.context.p1" = "PBL is what makes Ortega efficient: the method does not spend extra effort solving the first layer perfectly, because the final PBL step can fix both layers together.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.structure.b1" = "Ortega PBL has **6 cases**";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.structure.b2" = "Acts as the final step of Ortega";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.structure.b3" = "Handles upper-layer, lower-layer, and both-layer swaps";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.structure.b4" = "Recognition speed often matters more than raw algorithm speed";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.insights.p1" = "PBL is compact, but it can feel surprisingly slow if you only recognize the top layer. Strong Ortega solvers learn to read both layers quickly and predict AUF cleanly.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.insights.p2" = "Some PBL cases are also useful outside Ortega, because similar permutation ideas appear in EG and other 2x2 one-look systems.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.insights.p3" = "Practicing PBL from real Ortega scrambles is better than drilling solved setups only, because the bottom layer orientation and viewing angle affect how quickly the case appears.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.recognition.p1" = "Look for whether the top layer has a bar, whether the bottom layer has a bar, and whether the two layers need adjacent or diagonal swaps.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.recommended.p1" = "Recommended immediately after Ortega OLL. Without reliable PBL, Ortega will feel incomplete even if orientation is fast.";
"algs.info.ortegapbl.quote.p1" = "PBL is the payoff of Ortega: instead of forcing a solved first layer, you let the final algorithm clean up both layers at once.";
"algs.info.cll.title" = "About CLL";
"algs.info.cll.subtitle" = "One-look last layer with a solved first layer";
"algs.info.cll.overview.p1" = "**CLL**, or **Corners of the Last Layer**, solves the entire 2x2 last layer in one algorithm while preserving a solved first layer.";
"algs.info.cll.context.p1" = "CLL is one of the classic advanced 2x2 milestones. It is also closely related to EG: a solved first layer can be viewed as the EG-0 case family.";
"algs.info.cll.structure.b1" = "CLL has **40 cases**";
"algs.info.cll.structure.b2" = "Requires a solved first layer before the algorithm";
"algs.info.cll.structure.b3" = "Solves orientation and permutation together";
"algs.info.cll.structure.b4" = "Often easier to control than full EG because the bottom layer is solved";
"algs.info.cll.insights.p1" = "CLL gives a very clean solve flow: build a layer, recognize one last-layer case, and finish. The first layer takes more work than an Ortega face, but the last step is more powerful.";
"algs.info.cll.insights.p2" = "Many cubers learn CLL before EG because recognition is more stable. You can treat it as the center of the 2x2 advanced ecosystem rather than just another formula set.";
"algs.info.cll.insights.p3" = "Good CLL practice should include first-layer planning. If the layer takes too long, the one-look last layer will not actually save much time.";
"algs.info.cll.recognition.p1" = "Recognition combines last-layer orientation shape with corner permutation. Most solvers first identify the orientation family, then confirm which permutation variant is present.";
"algs.info.cll.recommended.p1" = "Recommended after Ortega is comfortable and you want a serious 2x2 upgrade without immediately taking on the full EG learning load.";
"algs.info.cll.quote.p1" = "CLL is where 2x2 starts to feel less like a reduced 3x3 and more like its own one-look puzzle.";
"algs.info.eg1.title" = "About EG-1";
"algs.info.eg1.subtitle" = "EG cases with an adjacent bottom-layer swap";
"algs.info.eg1.overview.p1" = "**EG-1** is part of the 2x2 EG method. It finishes the cube in one algorithm from a built face where the bottom layer has an adjacent swap relationship rather than being solved.";
"algs.info.eg1.context.p1" = "The EG method, associated with Erik Akkersdijk and Gunnar Krig, was proposed as a two-step 2x2 speed method: build a face, then solve the entire cube with one algorithm. EG-1 is one of its major case families.";
"algs.info.eg1.structure.b1" = "EG-1 has **40 cases**";
"algs.info.eg1.structure.b2" = "Uses a built face, not necessarily a solved layer";
"algs.info.eg1.structure.b3" = "Bottom-layer state is an adjacent swap pattern";
"algs.info.eg1.structure.b4" = "High payoff, but recognition and AUF discipline are demanding";
"algs.info.eg1.insights.p1" = "EG-1 is attractive because many scrambles have easy faces. If you can see the face and final case during inspection, the solve can become extremely direct.";
"algs.info.eg1.insights.p2" = "The cost is that the first face must be aligned correctly for the algorithm. This makes pre-AUF and post-AUF more important than in CLL.";
"algs.info.eg1.insights.p3" = "Many solvers use EG-1 selectively before learning full EG. It is often the most practical first step beyond CLL because adjacent bottom states are common and readable.";
"algs.info.eg1.recognition.p1" = "Recognition starts from the bottom face relationship, then uses the upper-layer corner pattern to choose the exact algorithm.";
"algs.info.eg1.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for advanced 2x2 solvers who already know CLL and want to convert easier face builds into full one-look finishes.";
"algs.info.eg1.quote.p1" = "EG-1 rewards a different kind of speed: not just fast turning, but seeing a useful unsolved face as a complete solve waiting to happen.";
"algs.info.eg2.title" = "About EG-2";
"algs.info.eg2.subtitle" = "EG cases with a diagonal bottom-layer swap";
"algs.info.eg2.overview.p1" = "**EG-2** completes the 2x2 from a built face where the bottom layer has a diagonal swap relationship. It is the other large EG family beyond CLL and EG-1.";
"algs.info.eg2.context.p1" = "EG-2 belongs to the full EG method, but many solvers learn it later than EG-1 because some cases can be avoided or converted in practical solves.";
"algs.info.eg2.structure.b1" = "EG-2 has **40 cases**";
"algs.info.eg2.structure.b2" = "Uses a built face with a diagonal bottom-layer swap";
"algs.info.eg2.structure.b3" = "Completes the cube in one algorithm from that state";
"algs.info.eg2.structure.b4" = "Usually treated as a late advanced 2x2 set";
"algs.info.eg2.insights.p1" = "EG-2 can be very powerful, but it is less forgiving than CLL because the bottom layer is not solved and the case must be aligned correctly.";
"algs.info.eg2.insights.p2" = "Some cubers delay full EG-2 and instead use CLL-like alternatives or first-face choices that avoid the hardest cases. That is a valid practical path, not a failure to learn the full system.";
"algs.info.eg2.insights.p3" = "If you do learn EG-2, drill it from inspection-style face builds rather than only from isolated alg trainers. The value appears when the face and last step are planned together.";
"algs.info.eg2.recognition.p1" = "Recognize the diagonal relationship on the bottom face first, then identify the upper-layer corner case and required AUF.";
"algs.info.eg2.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for serious 2x2 solvers who already know CLL and EG-1 and want a more complete one-look toolkit.";
"algs.info.eg2.quote.p1" = "EG-2 is not just more algorithms; it is a commitment to treating the first face as a flexible launch point instead of a solved layer.";
"algs.info.ollparity.title" = "About 4x4 OLL Parity";
"algs.info.ollparity.subtitle" = "Orientation parity after 4x4 reduction";
"algs.info.ollparity.overview.p1" = "**4x4 OLL Parity** is the familiar last-layer state where one dedge appears flipped after the puzzle has been reduced to a 3x3-like state.";
"algs.info.ollparity.context.p1" = "Parity appears on even-layer cubes because center and edge pairing can create states that cannot occur on a real 3x3. OLL parity is the orientation-looking version of that problem.";
"algs.info.ollparity.structure.b1" = "This library covers **3 practical OLL parity algorithms**";
"algs.info.ollparity.structure.b2" = "Usually appears after edge pairing and before normal OLL/PLL completion";
"algs.info.ollparity.structure.b3" = "Fixes the reduced edge orientation state";
"algs.info.ollparity.structure.b4" = "Wide turns and slice control matter more than memorizing many cases";
"algs.info.ollparity.insights.p1" = "OLL parity is not a normal OLL case. It is a reduction artifact, so the best solution is usually a dedicated parity algorithm rather than trying to reason like a 3x3.";
"algs.info.ollparity.insights.p2" = "Execution quality matters a lot because parity algorithms are long and slice-heavy. A stable grip and clean wide-turn rhythm are more useful than chasing obscure alternatives early.";
"algs.info.ollparity.insights.p3" = "Once the parity is fixed, the solve returns to ordinary 3x3-style last-layer logic.";
"algs.info.ollparity.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is straightforward: after reduction, the last layer looks like one edge pair is flipped or the OLL state is otherwise impossible on a 3x3.";
"algs.info.ollparity.recommended.p1" = "Recommended as soon as you start solving 4x4 with reduction or Yau. It is mandatory knowledge, not an optional advanced set.";
"algs.info.ollparity.quote.p1" = "4x4 OLL parity is the moment the reduced cube reminds you that it was never truly a 3x3.";
"algs.info.pllparity.title" = "About 4x4 PLL Parity";
"algs.info.pllparity.subtitle" = "Permutation parity after 4x4 reduction";
"algs.info.pllparity.overview.p1" = "**4x4 PLL Parity** is a last-layer permutation problem where the reduced cube reaches a swap state that would be impossible on a standard 3x3.";
"algs.info.pllparity.context.p1" = "Like OLL parity, PLL parity comes from reducing an even-layer puzzle. The centers and paired edges can be solved while leaving an odd permutation in the reduced 3x3 model.";
"algs.info.pllparity.structure.b1" = "This library covers **22 PLL parity cases and algorithms**";
"algs.info.pllparity.structure.b2" = "Often appears as a two-edge-pair swap or adjacent-looking last-layer impossibility";
"algs.info.pllparity.structure.b3" = "Usually solved before or during the final PLL phase";
"algs.info.pllparity.structure.b4" = "Many variants exist, but a small reliable toolkit is enough for most solvers";
"algs.info.pllparity.insights.p1" = "PLL parity is where reduction efficiency and 3x3 recognition meet. The case may look like a familiar PLL until one impossible swap remains.";
"algs.info.pllparity.insights.p2" = "Some solvers use one main algorithm for nearly everything; others learn adjacent, diagonal, or recognition-specific variants. The right choice depends on how much 4x4 you solve.";
"algs.info.pllparity.insights.p3" = "Because PLL parity often arrives near the end of the solve, calm execution is valuable. A lockup here can erase a lot of good work from earlier phases.";
"algs.info.pllparity.recognition.p1" = "Look for a reduced last layer where the remaining permutation would require swapping a single pair of dedges or otherwise violates normal 3x3 parity.";
"algs.info.pllparity.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for every 4x4 solver. Learn one dependable algorithm first, then add faster variants only if you solve the event often.";
"algs.info.pllparity.quote.p1" = "PLL parity is not a weird PLL; it is the price of pretending an even-layer cube became a 3x3.";
"algs.info.l2e.title" = "About 5x5 L2E";
"algs.info.l2e.subtitle" = "Last two edges in 5x5 reduction";
"algs.info.l2e.overview.p1" = "**L2E**, or **Last Two Edges**, is the final edge-pairing stage on 5x5. It resolves the last unsolved dedges before the puzzle can be solved like a 3x3.";
"algs.info.l2e.context.p1" = "In reduction and Yau-style 5x5 solving, most edges can be paired freely, but the final two often require specific cases. L2E is the compact algorithmic toolkit for that bottleneck.";
"algs.info.l2e.structure.b1" = "This library covers **13 L2E cases**";
"algs.info.l2e.structure.b2" = "Used near the end of edge pairing";
"algs.info.l2e.structure.b3" = "Aims to finish the final two dedges cleanly";
"algs.info.l2e.structure.b4" = "Good setup choices can reduce how many cases feel necessary";
"algs.info.l2e.insights.p1" = "L2E is less about a huge method and more about removing the last awkward edge-pairing stop. It keeps the solve from stalling right before the 3x3 phase.";
"algs.info.l2e.insights.p2" = "Many cases can be approached by understanding how wings pair and swap, not just by memorizing an image. That understanding helps on 6x6 and 7x7 too.";
"algs.info.l2e.insights.p3" = "The best practice is to drill L2E after realistic partial edge-pairing states, because setup and case selection are part of the skill.";
"algs.info.l2e.recognition.p1" = "Recognition focuses on how the two remaining wing pairs are split across the front, back, and slice positions, plus whether a short setup can turn the state into a familiar case.";
"algs.info.l2e.recommended.p1" = "Recommended once you can finish 5x5 centers and most edges comfortably but still lose time on the final two edges.";
"algs.info.l2e.quote.p1" = "L2E is the small gate between big-cube building and the familiar 3x3 finish.";
"algs.info.l2c.title" = "About 5x5 L2C";
"algs.info.l2c.subtitle" = "Last two centers in 5x5 center building";
"algs.info.l2c.overview.p1" = "**L2C**, or **Last Two Centers**, covers the final center-building cases on 5x5 before edge pairing or later reduction steps continue.";
"algs.info.l2c.context.p1" = "On 5x5, centers are fixed relative to each other, but building the last two centers efficiently can still be awkward. L2C algorithms give structure to those final center cases.";
"algs.info.l2c.structure.b1" = "This library covers **18 L2C cases**";
"algs.info.l2c.structure.b2" = "Used while completing the final two centers";
"algs.info.l2c.structure.b3" = "Preserves already built centers while arranging center pieces";
"algs.info.l2c.structure.b4" = "Mostly a reduction-efficiency set, not a last-layer set";
"algs.info.l2c.insights.p1" = "L2C matters because center building sets the rhythm for the rest of a 5x5 solve. If the last centers are clumsy, the solve loses flow before edge pairing even begins.";
"algs.info.l2c.insights.p2" = "Some solvers solve L2C mostly intuitively, while others prefer a small set of known cases. The best approach is the one that keeps lookahead and center preservation stable.";
"algs.info.l2c.insights.p3" = "Learning L2C also improves general big-cube center intuition, especially how pieces move through slices without destroying finished centers.";
"algs.info.l2c.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on the pattern of remaining center pieces on the last two center faces and whether they can be paired, swapped, or inserted with a clean slice sequence.";
"algs.info.l2c.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for 5x5 solvers who already understand center building but want fewer pauses on the final centers.";
"algs.info.l2c.quote.p1" = "Good L2C turns the last centers from a scramble of pieces into a short, planned construction step.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.title" = "About SQ1 Cube Shape";
"algs.info.sq1cs.subtitle" = "Returning Square-1 to cube shape";
"algs.info.sq1cs.overview.p1" = "**Square-1 Cube Shape** is the first major phase of most Square-1 methods. It turns the puzzle from an arbitrary shape back into a cube shape so later layer and permutation steps can be applied.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.context.p1" = "Cube shape is central to Square-1 because the puzzle changes shape while scrambling. Methods like Vandenbergh and Lin both depend on reaching cube shape before the more algorithmic endgame begins.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.structure.b1" = "Square-1 Cube Shape has **170 shape cases** in this classification";
"algs.info.sq1cs.structure.b2" = "Usually the first step of a Square-1 solve";
"algs.info.sq1cs.structure.b3" = "Shape names such as kite, fist, scallop, and shield are common recognition anchors";
"algs.info.sq1cs.structure.b4" = "Efficiency depends on recognizing shape families, not memorizing every path equally";
"algs.info.sq1cs.insights.p1" = "Cube shape is where Square-1 feels most different from NxN puzzles. You are not just moving pieces; you are controlling which turns are even possible.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.insights.p2" = "Strong solvers often think in shape transitions. Instead of seeing each state as a separate algorithm, they learn how common shapes flow into simpler shapes and then into cube shape.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.insights.p3" = "Because shape affects legal moves, deliberate practice with names and visual silhouettes pays off quickly.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on the top and bottom layer shape pair. Learning names like kite/kite or fist/scallop gives each state a verbal handle, which makes drilling much easier.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for every Square-1 solver before deep endgame algorithms. Without cube-shape fluency, the rest of the method cannot flow.";
"algs.info.sq1cs.quote.p1" = "Cube shape is the language of Square-1: once the shapes have names, the puzzle starts to become readable.";
"algs.info.sq1co.title" = "About SQ1 Corner Orientation";
"algs.info.sq1co.subtitle" = "Placing Square-1 corners on the correct layers";
"algs.info.sq1co.overview.p1" = "**SQ1 Corner Orientation** brings the corners to the correct layers after cube shape. In Vandenbergh-style solving, it is one of the early steps that prepares the puzzle for permutation.";
"algs.info.sq1co.context.p1" = "The Vandenbergh method describes the post-cube-shape flow as corners to correct layers, edges to correct layers, then corner and edge permutation. CO is the first of those layer-correction phases.";
"algs.info.sq1co.structure.b1" = "SQ1 CO has **7 cases**";
"algs.info.sq1co.structure.b2" = "Comes after cube shape";
"algs.info.sq1co.structure.b3" = "Separates corner-layer correction from later permutation";
"algs.info.sq1co.structure.b4" = "Small set, but important for method rhythm";
"algs.info.sq1co.insights.p1" = "CO is not glamorous, but it keeps the endgame organized. By placing corners on the right layers early, later CP cases become much easier to classify.";
"algs.info.sq1co.insights.p2" = "Because the set is small, the goal should be instant recognition and very stable slash timing.";
"algs.info.sq1co.insights.p3" = "Treat CO and EO as a pair of preparation steps: together they turn a shaped Square-1 into something ready for permutation.";
"algs.info.sq1co.recognition.p1" = "Recognition focuses on whether top and bottom layer corners belong on their current layers or need to be exchanged across the slash.";
"algs.info.sq1co.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for Square-1 solvers learning Vandenbergh or any similar layer-correction endgame.";
"algs.info.sq1co.quote.p1" = "CO is the quiet reset after cube shape: corners stop being shape pieces and become layer pieces again.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.title" = "About SQ1 Edge Orientation";
"algs.info.sq1eo.subtitle" = "Placing Square-1 edges on the correct layers";
"algs.info.sq1eo.overview.p1" = "**SQ1 Edge Orientation** brings the edges to the correct layers after cube shape and corner orientation. It prepares the puzzle for CP, EP, and parity handling.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.context.p1" = "In Vandenbergh-style Square-1, EO follows the same logic as CO: separate layer correction from permutation so the endgame can be solved in clean stages.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.structure.b1" = "SQ1 EO has **7 cases**";
"algs.info.sq1eo.structure.b2" = "Usually follows CO";
"algs.info.sq1eo.structure.b3" = "Completes layer correction for edges";
"algs.info.sq1eo.structure.b4" = "Good EO recognition makes CP and EP feel much less chaotic";
"algs.info.sq1eo.insights.p1" = "EO is where the Square-1 endgame starts to become predictable. Once edges are on the correct layers, permutation cases become much easier to reason about.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.insights.p2" = "The set is small enough that you should aim for automatic execution, but still practice it in real solve contexts because slash alignment matters.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.insights.p3" = "EO also helps you notice parity earlier, because the puzzle's layer structure becomes clearer after orientation steps are finished.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on which edges belong to the top or bottom layer and whether the current layer arrangement is already correct or needs a controlled swap.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for every Square-1 solver using a staged method such as Vandenbergh.";
"algs.info.sq1eo.quote.p1" = "After EO, Square-1 stops feeling shapeless and starts behaving like a permutation puzzle.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.title" = "About SQ1 Corner Permutation";
"algs.info.sq1cp.subtitle" = "Permuting Square-1 corners within their layers";
"algs.info.sq1cp.overview.p1" = "**SQ1 Corner Permutation** permutes the corners once cube shape and layer orientation are complete. It is the corner-solving part of the Square-1 endgame.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.context.p1" = "In Vandenbergh, CP comes after corners and edges have been placed on the correct layers. This separation keeps the case set relatively small and readable.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.structure.b1" = "SQ1 CP has **8 cases**";
"algs.info.sq1cp.structure.b2" = "Comes after CO and EO";
"algs.info.sq1cp.structure.b3" = "Solves corner order before edge permutation";
"algs.info.sq1cp.structure.b4" = "Often practiced with fingertrick-heavy slash sequences";
"algs.info.sq1cp.insights.p1" = "CP is a good example of why Square-1 methods are staged: once the pieces are on the correct layers, corner permutation becomes a compact problem.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.insights.p2" = "The main challenge is not volume but confidence. A small hesitation in CP often interrupts the solve more than the algorithm length itself.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.insights.p3" = "Good CP recognition also makes the transition into EP or parity smoother because you understand what remains after the corners are fixed.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.recognition.p1" = "Look at the corner order around each layer and identify whether the corners need adjacent swaps, diagonal swaps, or are already solved.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.recommended.p1" = "Recommended once CO and EO are stable. CP is one of the core Square-1 endgame sets, not an optional extra.";
"algs.info.sq1cp.quote.p1" = "CP is where Square-1 starts to look solved, even though the edges may still have the final word.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.title" = "About SQ1 Parity";
"algs.info.sq1parity.subtitle" = "Handling Square-1 parity in the endgame";
"algs.info.sq1parity.overview.p1" = "**SQ1 Parity** covers endgame states where the normal permutation flow needs a parity-specific fix, often around the edge permutation phase.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.context.p1" = "The Vandenbergh method lists parity as a final step, but it can also be combined with edge permutation. Modern Square-1 practice often treats parity as something to detect and integrate into the finish.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.structure.b1" = "This library covers **5 SQ1 parity cases**";
"algs.info.sq1parity.structure.b2" = "Usually appears after CP and around EP";
"algs.info.sq1parity.structure.b3" = "Can be solved separately or combined with edge permutation";
"algs.info.sq1parity.structure.b4" = "Recognition matters because parity changes which EP finish is appropriate";
"algs.info.sq1parity.insights.p1" = "Parity is one reason Square-1 endgames can feel slippery. The puzzle may look close to solved, but a normal EP solution is not enough.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.insights.p2" = "Learning parity as a small set is useful, but the real goal is knowing when it should affect your EP choice.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.insights.p3" = "If you later learn Lin or more advanced Square-1 systems, parity awareness becomes even more important because it changes the PLL-style finish.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.recognition.p1" = "Recognition usually comes from the remaining edge permutation after CP: if the edge state cannot be solved by a normal EP flow, parity handling is needed.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.recommended.p1" = "Recommended after basic CP and EP are understandable. Do not leave parity as a mysterious emergency algorithm forever.";
"algs.info.sq1parity.quote.p1" = "SQ1 parity is not just an exception; it is part of the endgame language.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.title" = "About SQ1 Edge Permutation";
"algs.info.sq1ep.subtitle" = "Final edge permutation in Square-1";
"algs.info.sq1ep.overview.p1" = "**SQ1 Edge Permutation** solves the remaining edge order near the end of a Square-1 solve, usually after cube shape, orientation, and corner permutation are complete.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.context.p1" = "Vandenbergh-style methods often finish with EP and parity, and those steps may be separated or combined depending on the solver's algorithm set.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.structure.b1" = "SQ1 EP has **49 cases**";
"algs.info.sq1ep.structure.b2" = "Final major phase of the Vandenbergh endgame";
"algs.info.sq1ep.structure.b3" = "Interacts closely with parity";
"algs.info.sq1ep.structure.b4" = "A larger set than CO, EO, or CP, so subset practice helps";
"algs.info.sq1ep.insights.p1" = "EP is where Square-1 endgame volume starts to matter. Earlier steps are compact, but final edge cases can branch more widely.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.insights.p2" = "Rather than treating all EP cases equally, it helps to group them by swaps and cycles. This makes recognition more structured and reduces the feeling of a giant list.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.insights.p3" = "Parity-aware EP is especially valuable because it avoids treating parity as a separate panic button at the very end.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.recognition.p1" = "Recognition focuses on the remaining edge cycles on the top and bottom layers, plus whether the state should be handled as a normal EP or parity-involved finish.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.recommended.p1" = "Recommended after CO, EO, and CP are stable. EP is the natural next place to improve Square-1 endgame speed.";
"algs.info.sq1ep.quote.p1" = "EP is the last layer of meaning in Square-1: once you can read the edge cycles, the finish stops feeling random.";
"algs.info.lin.title" = "About Lin";
"algs.info.lin.subtitle" = "A Square-1 endgame framework built around PLL-style finishes";
"algs.info.lin.overview.p1" = "**Lin** is an advanced Square-1 method family that restructures the endgame around parity-aware PLL-style stages instead of following the basic Vandenbergh finish exactly.";
"algs.info.lin.context.p1" = "Lin is part of modern Square-1 speedsolving's push toward faster, more direct finishes. It keeps the familiar cube-shape and orientation ideas, then changes how the final permutation work is organized.";
"algs.info.lin.structure.b1" = "Lin has **115 cases** in this library structure";
"algs.info.lin.structure.b2" = "Includes Lin PLL, Lin parity PLL, and Lin PLL +1 style finishes";
"algs.info.lin.structure.b3" = "Builds on Square-1 orientation and permutation fundamentals";
"algs.info.lin.structure.b4" = "Best learned after Vandenbergh endgame recognition is already comfortable";
"algs.info.lin.insights.p1" = "Lin is not a beginner replacement for Square-1 basics. It makes more sense once you already understand why CO, EO, CP, EP, and parity exist.";
"algs.info.lin.insights.p2" = "The benefit is a more unified finish: instead of treating every endgame exception separately, Lin groups many cases into PLL-like recognition families.";
"algs.info.lin.insights.p3" = "Because the set is large, selective learning is reasonable. Start with cases that appear often or replace endgame sequences you already dislike.";
"algs.info.lin.recognition.p1" = "Recognition depends on reading the remaining permutation and parity relationship after the setup phases, then choosing the appropriate Lin PLL-style finish.";
"algs.info.lin.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for advanced Square-1 solvers who already have a reliable Vandenbergh-style solve and want a more powerful endgame.";
"algs.info.lin.quote.p1" = "Lin turns Square-1's messy-looking finish into something closer to a last-layer language.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.title" = "About Megaminx EO";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.subtitle" = "Edge orientation in Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx EO** orients the last-layer edges as the first step of the common 4-look Megaminx last layer.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.context.p1" = "Megaminx last layer is often taught in a 4LLL structure: orient edges, orient corners, permute edges, then permute corners. EO is the small entry point into that system.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.structure.b1" = "Megaminx EO has **3 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.structure.b2" = "First step of Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.structure.b3" = "Uses edge orientation patterns similar in spirit to 3x3 OLL edges";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.structure.b4" = "Very low algorithm count, high practical value";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.insights.p1" = "EO is tiny, but it defines the rest of the last layer. Once edges are oriented, corner orientation becomes cleaner and the solve enters a structured path.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.insights.p2" = "Many 3x3 ideas transfer here, but the five-sided last layer changes recognition angles and fingertricks.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.insights.p3" = "Practice EO together with CO so the first two looks of 4LLL feel like one continuous orientation phase.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on how many last-layer edges show the last-layer color correctly relative to the top center.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for every Megaminx solver learning beyond beginner last-layer methods.";
"algs.info.megaminxeo.quote.p1" = "Megaminx EO is small enough to learn in a sitting, but important enough to shape the whole last layer.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.title" = "About Megaminx CO";
"algs.info.megaminxco.subtitle" = "Corner orientation in Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxco.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx CO** orients the last-layer corners after edge orientation. It is the second look in the common 4LLL Megaminx last-layer structure.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.context.p1" = "Speedsolving resources often describe Megaminx 4LLL as EO, CO, EP, and CP. CO is the corner-orientation counterpart to EO and resembles OCLL ideas from 3x3, adapted to a five-corner last layer.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.structure.b1" = "Megaminx CO has **16 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxco.structure.b2" = "Usually follows EO";
"algs.info.megaminxco.structure.b3" = "Preserves edge orientation while orienting corners";
"algs.info.megaminxco.structure.b4" = "A manageable middle step before larger PLL-style sets";
"algs.info.megaminxco.insights.p1" = "CO is where Megaminx last layer starts to feel like its own puzzle. Some 3x3 OCLL habits transfer, but the case shapes and rotations are different enough to deserve separate practice.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.insights.p2" = "Because there are 16 cases, grouping by corner pattern is more effective than memorizing a flat list.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.insights.p3" = "Good CO execution sets up EP and CP cleanly, so do not ignore AUF and ending angle.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on the orientation pattern of the five last-layer corners after EO is solved.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.recommended.p1" = "Recommended after EO. It is one of the core sets for a stable Megaminx 4LLL.";
"algs.info.megaminxco.quote.p1" = "Megaminx CO is familiar enough to welcome 3x3 solvers, but different enough to punish autopilot.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.title" = "About Megaminx EP";
"algs.info.megaminxep.subtitle" = "Edge permutation in Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxep.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx EP** permutes the last-layer edges after orientation is complete, leaving corner permutation as the final 4LLL step.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.context.p1" = "In Megaminx 4LLL, EP is deliberately small: orient everything first, then use a compact edge-permutation set before finishing corners.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.structure.b1" = "Megaminx EP has **5 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxep.structure.b2" = "Third step of common Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxep.structure.b3" = "Permutes edges while leaving corners for CP";
"algs.info.megaminxep.structure.b4" = "Small set, but recognition across five edges takes practice";
"algs.info.megaminxep.insights.p1" = "EP is one of the highest-value Megaminx sets because it is small and appears in every 4LLL solve.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.insights.p2" = "Since only five edges are involved, the cases can be understood as cycles rather than random patterns.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.insights.p3" = "Clean EP often determines how smoothly you enter CP, especially if your final AUF planning is weak.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.recognition.p1" = "Look for solved edge positions around the last layer and identify the remaining cycle or swap pattern.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.recommended.p1" = "Recommended after EO and CO. It is small enough that full EP should be an early Megaminx LL goal.";
"algs.info.megaminxep.quote.p1" = "Megaminx EP proves that not every useful set has to be large; sometimes five cases change the whole finish.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.title" = "About Megaminx CP";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.subtitle" = "Corner permutation in Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx CP** permutes the final five last-layer corners, completing the common 4-look Megaminx last layer.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.context.p1" = "CP is the fourth step of Megaminx 4LLL. Some solvers learn all cases, while others use commutators or a smaller practical subset.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.structure.b1" = "Megaminx CP has **15 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.structure.b2" = "Final step of Megaminx 4LLL";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.structure.b3" = "Permutes corners after edges are solved";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.structure.b4" = "Can be learned fully or supplemented with commutators";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.insights.p1" = "CP is often where Megaminx solves lose their last bit of flow. The puzzle is almost done, but five-corner recognition can still be slow.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.insights.p2" = "Learning CP as families helps a lot. You want to see solved corners, adjacent relationships, and cycles quickly instead of rotating repeatedly.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.insights.p3" = "For casual solvers, a commutator fallback is acceptable. For speed, a reliable CP set makes the finish feel much more decisive.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on which corners are already in place and how the remaining corners cycle around the last layer.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for Megaminx solvers who already know EO, CO, and EP and want a cleaner 4LLL finish.";
"algs.info.megaminxcp.quote.p1" = "Megaminx CP is the final handshake: the last layer is oriented and edged, but the corners still need to agree.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.title" = "About Megaminx OLL";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.subtitle" = "Combining EO and CO into one Megaminx orientation step";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx OLL** combines last-layer edge orientation and corner orientation into a single orientation step, reducing Megaminx last layer from 4LLL toward a more advanced 2-look or 3-look structure.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.context.p1" = "Speedsolving resources describe full Megaminx OLL as a very large advanced set. Even many strong solvers use only parts of it because the learning cost is high.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.structure.b1" = "Megaminx OLL has **178 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.structure.b2" = "Combines EO and CO";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.structure.b3" = "Part of advanced 2LLL-style Megaminx approaches";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.structure.b4" = "Large optional set; partial learning is normal";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.insights.p1" = "Megaminx OLL is powerful but expensive. Its value depends on whether the reduced look count actually beats spending that practice time on F2L, S2L, and 4LLL execution.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.insights.p2" = "Partial OLL can still be useful. High-frequency or ergonomic cases can improve solves without demanding the full set.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.insights.p3" = "Because Megaminx has more last-layer pieces than 3x3, recognition is the real bottleneck. The algorithm count is only half the challenge.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.recognition.p1" = "Recognition combines the edge orientation pattern and the five-corner orientation pattern into one case.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.recommended.p1" = "Recommended only after Megaminx 4LLL is strong and you specifically want to invest in advanced last-layer compression.";
"algs.info.megaminxoll.quote.p1" = "Megaminx OLL is a luxury set: beautiful when it works, but only worth it when the basics are already sharp.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.title" = "About Megaminx PLL";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.subtitle" = "Combining EP and CP into one Megaminx permutation step";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.overview.p1" = "**Megaminx PLL** permutes last-layer edges and corners together after orientation is complete, replacing separate EP and CP steps with one larger set.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.context.p1" = "Megaminx PLL is the key set in 3LLL approaches: EO, CO, then a combined PLL. It is large, but some 3x3 PLL ideas and algorithms transfer in modified form.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.structure.b1" = "Megaminx PLL has **151 cases**";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.structure.b2" = "Combines EP and CP";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.structure.b3" = "Used after last-layer orientation is solved";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.structure.b4" = "Large advanced set; many solvers learn useful subsets first";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.insights.p1" = "Megaminx PLL gives a clear payoff: one permutation step instead of two. The tradeoff is a much heavier recognition and memorization load.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.insights.p2" = "Because many 3x3 PLL concepts transfer, this set can feel more approachable than its size suggests, but only if your Megaminx orientation steps are already automatic.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.insights.p3" = "Partial PLL is common. Learn the cases that replace slow EP+CP combinations before chasing completion.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.recognition.p1" = "Recognition reads both edge and corner permutation around the five-sided last layer, usually starting from solved blocks or obvious cycles.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for advanced Megaminx solvers who already have reliable 4LLL and want to reduce last-layer looks.";
"algs.info.megaminxpll.quote.p1" = "Megaminx PLL is the advanced solver's bargain: fewer looks, more recognition pressure.";
"algs.info.l3e.title" = "About Pyraminx L3E";
"algs.info.l3e.subtitle" = "Last-layer finish with three remaining edges";
"algs.info.l3e.overview.p1" = "**Pyraminx L3E** solves the last layer when three edges remain to be resolved after the earlier block or layer work is done.";
"algs.info.l3e.context.p1" = "Many Pyraminx methods reduce the puzzle to a small last-layer edge problem. L3E is one compact way to finish from that stage.";
"algs.info.l3e.structure.b1" = "Pyraminx L3E has **5 cases**";
"algs.info.l3e.structure.b2" = "Small last-layer edge set";
"algs.info.l3e.structure.b3" = "Usually appears after a mostly solved body";
"algs.info.l3e.structure.b4" = "Fast recognition and low regrip execution are more important than set size";
"algs.info.l3e.insights.p1" = "Pyraminx rewards lookahead and turning fluidity heavily, so L3E should not be learned as isolated formulas only.";
"algs.info.l3e.insights.p2" = "The small case count makes it tempting to rush, but the real goal is choosing the right angle and preserving TPS through the final moves.";
"algs.info.l3e.insights.p3" = "L3E also helps build intuition for larger last-edge systems because the same cycle ideas appear again.";
"algs.info.l3e.recognition.p1" = "Recognition is based on which three last-layer edges remain unsolved and how their colors cycle around the top.";
"algs.info.l3e.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for Pyraminx solvers who know a basic method and want a cleaner last-layer finish.";
"algs.info.l3e.quote.p1" = "L3E is small, but on Pyraminx small sets can decide whether the solve ends smoothly or stutters.";
"algs.info.l4e.title" = "About Pyraminx L4E";
"algs.info.l4e.subtitle" = "Last four edges in V-first style Pyraminx solving";
"algs.info.l4e.overview.p1" = "**Pyraminx L4E** solves the final four edges, commonly associated with V-first style solving where the last phase is reduced to a four-edge problem.";
"algs.info.l4e.context.p1" = "V-first approaches build a V shape first, then solve the remaining edges. L4E is the algorithmic finish that makes that method competitive.";
"algs.info.l4e.structure.b1" = "Pyraminx L4E has **37 cases**";
"algs.info.l4e.structure.b2" = "Core finish for V-first style methods";
"algs.info.l4e.structure.b3" = "Larger and more advanced than L3E";
"algs.info.l4e.structure.b4" = "Most valuable when paired with strong inspection planning";
"algs.info.l4e.insights.p1" = "L4E is powerful because it lets the earlier solve be planned around a clean V, then finishes the remaining edges in one structured phase.";
"algs.info.l4e.insights.p2" = "The learning load is larger than basic Pyraminx last layers, so recognition families matter. Treat cases as edge cycles and swaps rather than disconnected pictures.";
"algs.info.l4e.insights.p3" = "Good L4E practice should include starts from real V-first solves, because the transition into the case is part of the method.";
"algs.info.l4e.recognition.p1" = "Recognition reads the four remaining edge positions and orientations after the V is formed.";
"algs.info.l4e.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for Pyraminx solvers who want to explore V-first or more advanced last-edge systems after basic methods are stable.";
"algs.info.l4e.quote.p1" = "L4E turns Pyraminx from a tiny beginner puzzle into a planning puzzle with a sharp algorithmic finish.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.title" = "About Sarah's Advanced";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.subtitle" = "Advanced Skewb finish in Sarah's Method";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.overview.p1" = "**Sarah's Advanced** is the advanced version of Sarah's Method for Skewb. After solving the first layer, it solves the remaining corners and centers together.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.context.p1" = "Sarah's Method, invented by Sarah Strong, is widely regarded as the main speedsolving method for Skewb. The advanced variation is also known as the L2L-style finish.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.structure.b1" = "Sarah's Advanced has **131 cases** in this library structure";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.structure.b2" = "Starts after the first layer is solved";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.structure.b3" = "Combines remaining corner and center solving";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.structure.b4" = "Large set; selective learning is practical";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.insights.p1" = "Skewb is turn-light but recognition-heavy. Sarah's Advanced pushes more solving into one final step, so the case count rises quickly.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.insights.p2" = "The method is powerful because Skewb's structure allows very short finishes when the case is known. The challenge is seeing the case without over-rotating.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.insights.p3" = "A practical path is to learn the common or ergonomic cases first, then expand as your first-layer planning becomes more consistent.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.recognition.p1" = "Recognition starts after the first layer: read the remaining corner pattern and center positions together, since the advanced finish solves both.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.recommended.p1" = "Recommended for Skewb solvers who already know Sarah's Beginner or Intermediate and want a deeper, faster finishing system.";
"algs.info.sarahsadvanced.quote.p1" = "Sarah's Advanced is Skewb's version of going deep: fewer steps, more responsibility in recognition.";
